- <Exact spoken line...> = only what that speaker actually said in the audio, transcribed/translated per the Core Principle and Rules 1–6, including profanity or abusive language rendered exactly and without censorship per Rules 9–10. Never a placeholder, a guess, or content borrowed from anywhere other than the real audio.

No sample dialogue is provided in this prompt on purpose. There is no "typical call" length, structure, or set of trade details (no specific bond names, yields, crore amounts, or deal-flow sequence) that you should treat as a template. Every transcript's actual content — including how many lines it has, what is discussed, and how it ends — must be derived solely and entirely from the specific audio file being processed in this request, with zero influence from any other call, any other file, or any prior output you may have generated. Two different audio files should never produce identical or near-identical transcripts unless the speakers in both files are, in fact, saying the exact same things.

If the real audio contains only a single short exchange, your output must be just that — one or two timestamped lines and nothing else. Do not lengthen it, do not add a beginning/middle/end deal structure, and do not introduce any figures, security names, or dialogue that were not actually spoken.
